{"content":[{"c":["BETH-EDEN"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Small Aramean (Syrian) state north of Damascus. Assyria conquered Beth-eden and deported its people to Kir (",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.16.9","r":"2Kgs.16.9","rawR":"2Kgs.16.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.16.9","value":"2Kgs.16.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 16:9"},"), fulfilling the prediction in ",{"href":"?bref=Amos.1.5","r":"Amos.1.5","rawR":"Amos.1.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Amos.1.5","value":"Amos.1.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"Amos 1:5"}," (",{"c":["kjv"],"sourceClass":"sc","t":"span"}," “house of Eden”). Beth-eden, which means “house of delight,” is sometimes equated with the Eden of ",{"href":"?bref=Ezek.27.23","r":"Ezek.27.23","rawR":"Ezek.27.23","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ezek.27.23","value":"Ezek.27.23"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ezekiel 27:23"},", the location of which is unknown. Its inhabitants are referred to as the “children of Eden” in ",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.19.12","r":"2Kgs.19.12","rawR":"2Kgs.19.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.19.12","value":"2Kgs.19.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kings 19:12"}," (",{"c":["kjv"],"sourceClass":"sc","t":"span"},")."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"BethEden","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"2 Kgs 16:9","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Kgs.16.9","rawQueryValue":"2Kgs.16.9","sourceRefExact":"2Kgs.16.9"},{"anchorText":"Amos 1:5","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Amos.1.5","rawQueryValue":"Amos.1.5","sourceRefExact":"Amos.1.5"},{"anchorText":"Ezekiel 27:23","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Ezek.27.23","rawQueryValue":"Ezek.27.23","sourceRefExact":"Ezek.27.23"},{"anchorText":"2 Kings 19:12","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Kgs.19.12","rawQueryValue":"